<h2>The counterargument: the even split myth</h2>

<p>The most common objection to the conservative negative split strategy is looking at the elites. The runners who win in Valencia run an <em>even split</em> — they nail the first half in 62 minutes and the second half in 62 minutes.</p>

<p>This is a fact, but it does not apply to your race. Elite athletes complete a marathon in just over two hours. At that level, they are running at intensities very close to their critical threshold for the entire event. Their limiter is purely metabolic.</p>

<p>For an amateur runner who takes three or four hours, the primary limiter in the final 10 kilometers is not the cardiovascular system, it is peripheral muscle fatigue. The mechanical damage of sustaining impact against the tarmac for over three hours destroys running economy.</p>

<p>Starting a few seconds slower in the first half drastically reduces that early peak in muscle damage. It protects you. It allows your legs to keep responding to your brain's commands at kilometer 35 (mile 22). The even split is the optimal strategy in a lab; the conservative negative split is the strategy that survives the tarmac.</p>
